dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hey, christopher, hey, theresa. so, how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing okay.
[theresa] i'm doing okay.
[doctor] good. well, let's get started. so, i know it's been a little while since we've seen each other. how's everything been going?
[patient] it's been going good.
[theresa] it's been going good.
[doctor] good. so, let's talk a little bit about your hydrocephalus. how's everything been going with that?
[patient] it's been good. i've been able to walk to the dining room just fine. i could not before the surgery.
[theresa] yeah, and he's been able to walk around here without his walker.
[doctor] that's great. and have you noticed any improvement with your balance?
[patient] yeah, i have. i'm not as wobbly as i was before.
[theresa] yeah, that's true. he's been able to walk around here without falling.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any improvement with your bladder control?
[patient] no, i still have to wear a diaper.
[theresa] yeah, he still has to wear a diaper.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any improvement with your thinking?
[patient] yeah, i have. i feel like i can have a normal conversation with him again.
[theresa] yeah, it's like we can have a conversation without him not remembering what we're talking about.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any headaches?
[patient] no.
[theresa] no.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any pain at the shunt site?
[patient] no.
[theresa] no.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your medical history. i know you've had some urinary incontinence. have you had any problems with genital sores, decreased or painful output?
[patient] no.
[theresa] no.
[doctor] okay. have you had any problems with headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations?
[patient] no.
[theresa] no.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any problems with depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings?
[patient] no.
[theresa] no.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your vitals. your blood pressure is 124 over 80. your heart rate is 64. your respiratory rate is 18. your weight is 174 pounds. and your pain is 0 out of 10.
[patient] okay.
[theresa] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your physical exam. on your head and face, the shunt site is clean, dry, and intact. on your neurological exam, your mental status is normal. you were able to spell and calculate and you were able to get two of the three memory words with cuing and the third one with multiple choice. that's a slight improvement over your initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling and at that time, you were unable to remember any memory words with cuing and only one with multiple choice. on your gait, you were tested without an assistive device and you received a gait score of 6 to 8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28. that's a slight improvement from your initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28. on your cranial nerves, your pupils are equal. your extraocular movements are intact. your face is symmetric. you do not have dysarthria. on your motor exam, you have normal bulk and strength. on your coordination, you're slow for finger-to-nose.
[patient] okay.
[theresa] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your ct scan of the head from 10/15/2020. it shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of munro of 4.6 centimeters with a third ventricular contour that is flat with the span of 10 millimeters. by my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. that was not noticed by the radiologist who stated no extra axial fluid collections. there is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.
[patient] okay.
[theresa] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your assessment. you have made some improvement with your hydrocephalus since shunt surgery. there is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. i do not believe that this tiny amount of fluid is symptomatic and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the ct scan.
[patient] okay.
[theresa] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your plan. i'm going to leave the setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing the shunt. we'll follow up in three months. and i'm going to recommend that you continue to wear a diaper. i'm also going to recommend that you continue to avoid heavy lifting, bending, or straining. and we'll continue to follow up with you in three months to see how you're doing.
[patient] okay.
[theresa] okay.
[doctor] okay. and i would like to recommend that you enroll in a driver's safety program. i know you've been asking about driving. i do not think you will be able to pass a driver's safety evaluation at this time, but occasionally patients surprise me by improving over 9 to 12 months, so i would be happy to recommend that you enroll in a driver's safety program. you're always welcome to enroll in a driver's safety program without my recommendation, but i do not think you have the capability of passing such a rigorous test at this time.
[patient] okay.
[theresa] okay.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to prescribe you a follow-up appointment in three months. }}<|eot_id|>
