dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, amanda.
[patient] hi, doctor.
[doctor] how are you doing?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm just frustrated.
[doctor] okay. what's frustrating you?
[patient] my blood pressure. it's just so high.
[doctor] okay. well, let's take a look at it. so, i see you were here last week, and i put you on hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 milligrams once daily and lisinopril 20 milligrams once daily. how have you been doing since then?
[patient] well, i've been taking them, and my blood pressure is still high. it's just been really high.
[doctor] okay. what's your blood pressure been?
[patient] it's been 135-145 or 150 over 95-110.
[doctor] okay. well, let's take a look at your blood pressure right now. (BP: 140/90). okay. and how have you been feeling? have you been experiencing any side effects from the medications?
[patient] well, i have been experiencing some headaches. i've been having some ear problems. i've been having some fatigue. and i've been having some lethargy.
[doctor] okay. have you been taking the full dose of lisinopril?
[patient] no, i've been taking half of it.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk about your drinking. you've been drinking quite a bit. how many times a week do you drink?
[patient] i drink about four to five times a week.
[doctor] okay. and have you been able to cut back on your drinking?
[patient] no, i haven't. i've been trying, but i haven't been able to cut back.
[doctor] okay. well, i think you know that you need to cut back on your drinking. your blood pressure is very high, and i think if you were able to stop drinking, your blood pressure would come down significantly.
[patient] i know. i've been trying.
[doctor] okay. well, let's talk about the naltrexone. have you thought about that?
[patient] yes, i have. i've been looking into it. i've been looking at the naltrexone injections.
[doctor] okay. well, i think that would be a great option for you. i'll start you on the naltrexone. i'll give you a prescription for the injections. we'll start you on it on may 17th. and i want you to take it every week. have you thought about what you're going to do about your drinking?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. well, let's talk about that. i think you need to stop drinking. i think you need to quit drinking. i think if you can quit drinking, your blood pressure will come down. and i think it would be a great idea for you to just stop drinking. have you thought about that?
[patient] i've thought about it, but i'm just so addicted.
[doctor] okay. well, let's talk about the naltrexone. the naltrexone is a medication that can help you quit drinking. it's an injection that you'll get every week. it's going to make you feel a little bit sick when you drink. and it's going to make you not want to drink. and i think that will really help you quit drinking.
[patient] that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm going to start you on the naltrexone. i'm going to give you a prescription for the injections. and i want you to start it on may 17th. and then we'll follow up in a month. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. great. so, let's talk about your blood pressure. i want you to continue taking the lisinopril and the hydrochlorothiazide. i want you to continue taking those every day. and i want you to try to stop drinking. i want you to try to quit drinking. and i want you to come back in a month to follow up. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. great. and i want you to call the clinic with any questions. and i want you to make a follow-up appointment for the week of june 14th. okay?
[patient] okay. okay. }}<|eot_id|>
