dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hey, how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing well, thanks. how about you?
[doctor] good. good. so, you're doing okay? you know, i know you had that heart attack six months ago, and i'm glad you're doing well.
[patient] yeah, i'm doing well.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm glad to hear that. and i know you're from the west coast, so i'm a 49ers fan, so i'm glad to hear that you're from the west coast. but i'm glad to hear that you're doing well.
[patient] yeah, i'm doing well.
[doctor] okay. so, i know you had that heart attack six months ago. and i know that you've been doing well. and i know that you had a stent put in. and i know that you've been doing well. so, let's talk about your medications. so, what medications are you on?
[patient] i'm on aspirin, brilinta, fish oil, crestor, and metoprolol 50 mg once a day.
[doctor] okay. so, have you been taking those consistently?
[patient] yeah, i've been taking them consistently.
[doctor] okay. good. so, let's talk about your review of systems. how are you doing? how are your muscles doing? do you have any muscle aches?
[patient] no, i don't have any muscle aches.
[doctor] okay. and how about your nerves? do you have any numbness or tingling in your hands or feet?
[patient] no, i don't have any numbness or tingling.
[doctor] okay. and how about your depression? are you feeling down or blue?
[patient] i'm feeling a little bit down. i'm just having a hard time adjusting since my heart attack.
[doctor] okay. well, let's talk about that. let's talk about your depression. i know that it can be tough to adjust after a heart attack. but i want you to know that i'm here for you, and we can work through this together. have you been feeling suicidal or homicidal?
[patient] no, i have not been feeling suicidal or homicidal.
[doctor] okay. okay. and have you been having any thoughts about hurting yourself or others?
[patient] no, i have not been having any thoughts about hurting myself or others.
[doctor] okay. okay. so, let's talk about your vital signs. so, your blood pressure is looking good. your heart rate is looking good. and your respiratory rate is looking good. so, let's talk about your physical exam. so, your neck is looking good. your respiratory exam is looking good. your heart exam is looking good. you have a slight 3/6 systolic ejection murmur at the left base, which is something that we've seen in the past. and your lower extremities are looking good. you have one plus non-pitting edema in your lower extremities, which is something that we've seen in the past. so, let's talk about your lab results. so, your creatinine levels are looking stable. so, let's talk about your assessment and plan. so, i'm going to review my notes. so, i know that you had a heart attack six months ago. and i know that you've been doing well. and i know that you've been compliant with your medications. and i know that your stents are in place. and i know that they typically do not require replacement. but i do want you to know that it is common to experience dizziness when you stand quickly. and that should resolve once you're adjusted to your medications. and i do want you to know that cardiac rehab is going to be beneficial in helping you become more active and confident with exercising. and i do want you to watch for symptoms such as chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion. if you experience those symptoms, we will need to do a stress test. so, let's talk about your medical treatment. i'm going to refer you to cardiac rehab. i'm going to give you a refill of metoprolol 50 mg once a day. i'm going to give you a refill of crestor 40 mg once a day. and i'm going to advise you to continue taking aspirin daily long term as well as brilinta for the next six months. so, are you okay with that?
[patient] yeah, i'm okay with that.
[doctor] okay. and i'm also going to refer you to your nephrologist, dr. miller, because i know that you have stage three chronic kidney disease. so, let's talk about that. so, your most recent creatinine levels are stable. so, let's talk about your diabetes. so, your most recent hemoglobin a1c was 16. and i know that we've talked about the importance of diabetes management and how it plays a role in your coronary artery disease. so, let's talk about that. so, i know that your hemoglobin a1c goal is going to be six. so, let's talk about your medical treatment. i'm going to order a repeat hemoglobin a1c today. i'm going to refer you to a dietitian for diabetes education. and i'm going to contact your primary care physician regarding your insulin regimen. so, are you okay with that?
[patient] yeah, i'm okay with that.
[doctor] okay. and i'm also going to refer you to psychiatry today because i know that you're feeling a little bit down. and i know that you're having a hard time adjusting since your heart attack. and i know that you're lonely and you don't have a support system. so, are you okay with that?
[patient] yeah, i'm okay with that.
[doctor] okay. okay. so, are you okay with the recommended medical treatment plan?
[patient] yeah, i'm okay with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
