dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hey there, so, um, you know, i'm glad you came in today. you're a little bit early. i'm dr. martin. i'm a cardiologist. how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm having some chest pain.
[doctor] chest pain? what's been going on with that?
[patient] i've been having some chest pain on exertion.
[doctor] okay. and how long has this been going on?
[patient] um, a few weeks now.
[doctor] okay. and is it when you're walking, when you're running, or just doing something in particular?
[patient] um, it's when i'm walking. i'm having trouble with my legs.
[doctor] okay. and are you having any shortness of breath?
[patient] no, not really.
[doctor] okay. and are you having any nausea or vomiting or diarrhea?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. so, um, i'm going to go ahead and do a physical exam. let me go ahead and listen to your heart. everything looks good. your heart sounds normal. i'm going to listen to your lungs. everything looks good. you have a good respiratory exam. i'm going to go ahead and listen to your heart again. everything looks good. i'm going to listen to your abdomen. everything looks good. i'm going to go ahead and do a quick examination of your neck. everything looks good. and your extremities look good. you do have some swelling in your ankles, which i think is related to your diabetes. i'm going to go ahead and order an ekg, and i'm also going to order a hemoglobin a1c. i'm going to go ahead and go over your medical history. you have a history of diabetes type 2, and you have not been formally diagnosed with hypertension, but your blood pressures have been trending high. do you have any questions about that?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any family history of heart disease?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. are you currently taking any medications?
[patient] i'm taking metformin 500 mg twice daily.
[doctor] okay. all right. let me go ahead and review your medical history. um, i'm going to go ahead and go over your medical history. so, you have a history of diabetes type 2, and you have not been formally diagnosed with hypertension, but your blood pressures have been trending high. and you have a family history of heart disease. let me go ahead and review your review of systems. do you have any chest pain?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. do you have any shortness of breath?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. do you have any nausea or vomiting or diarrhea?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. all right. i'm going to go ahead and go over your physical exam. your neck exam is normal. your respiratory exam is normal. your cardiovascular exam is normal. your abdominal exam is normal. your musculoskeletal exam is normal. you do have 1+ nonpitting edema in your ankles. okay. i'm going to go ahead and review your results. i've reviewed your ekg, and it looks normal. your hemoglobin a1c is 7.0. let me go ahead and go over your assessment and plan. i'm going to go ahead and assess you with chest pain. so, considering your history of diabetes, possible history of hypertension, and family history of heart disease, i suspect your chest pain is likely unstable angina. your recent ekg was unremarkable, and there are no other signs of heart attack, but i'm worried you may have some sort of a blockage causing your pain. i'm going to go ahead and go over your patient education and counseling. we discussed proper protocol for sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain. i advised you to go to the emergency department if the medication is not effective after approximately 5 minutes. i'm going to go ahead and go over your medical treatment. we will have you start a regimen of baby aspirin at 81 mg daily. i've sent in a prescription today. i'm also going to have you start a regimen of nitroglycerin up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes as needed. and i'm going to have you follow up with cardiology for further evaluation. i'm also going to go ahead and assess you with type 2 diabetes. you appear to be doing well on your current regimen. i encouraged you to keep your appointment with ophthalmology for evaluation of diabetic retinopathy. i'm going to go ahead and go over your medical treatment. i'm going to put in an order for a repeat hemoglobin a1c to check for any needed medication adjustments. you will continue with metformin 500 mg twice daily and dietary modifications. i'm also going to go ahead and assess you with hypertension. several providers, including myself, have noted a trend of elevated blood pressures in your chart. i advised you to monitor your blood pressures at home over the next 2 weeks and report your readings to me via mychart. this should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to your new medications. we discussed the possible side effects of hydrochlorothiazide, and how this will hopefully reduce some of the swelling seen on physical exam. all of your questions were answered. i'm going to have you start hydrochlorothiazide at a low dose. and your instructions are to follow up in 2 weeks. is that okay with you?
